WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:06.930
In nineteen seventy-nine, two brothers from Texas walked into Wall Street with an idea so crazy that nobody believed they were serious.

2
00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:11.980
They decided that paper money was completely worthless, that the government was heading for bankruptcy,

3
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:18.620
and that the only safe place to put their wealth was inside cold, hard metal.

4
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.050
But they did not just buy a little bit.

5
00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:28.760
Over twelve months, these two brothers secretly bought one-third of all the privately held silver on planet Earth.

6
00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:30.890
They pushed the price of silver up by more than seven hundred percent.

7
00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:36.430
They caused people to tear down antique candelabras and melt family heirlooms on street corners.

8
00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:40.490
And then, just when they thought they owned the world,

9
00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:42.830
the men who ran the financial system did something the brothers never saw coming.

10
00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:46.090
They simply changed the rules.

11
00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:50.570
To understand how anyone could attempt something this absurd, you first have to meet their father.

12
00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:56.210
His name was Haroldson Lafayette Hunt, better known across Texas simply as H.L. Hunt.

13
00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:59.130
In the nineteen thirties, H.L.

14
00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.390
was quite literally the richest private citizen in the world.

15
00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:06.770
He started out as a professional poker player wandering through dusty gambling dens in Arkansas and Louisiana.

16
00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:12.180
One afternoon, he won a tiny slice of an oil lease in a card game.

17
00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:20.740
He took that lease, traded it for another, and eventually bought the legendary East Texas Oil Field from a broke wildcatter for pennies on the dollar. H.L.

18
00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.410
Hunt was brilliant, completely eccentric, and deeply paranoid.

19
00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:26.120
He carried his lunch in a brown paper bag, drove an old beat-up car,

20
00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:33.860
and firmly believed that paper money was a government scam designed to steal wealth from hardworking families.

21
00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.950
When he died in nineteen seventy-four, he left behind billions of dollars and a large family.

22
00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:42.710
Standing right at the head of the table was his eldest son,

23
00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:46.730
Nelson Bunker Hunt, alongside his younger brother, William Herbert Hunt.

24
00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:56.720
Bunker Hunt was a massive, quiet man who wore rumpled off-the-rack suits and looked more like a sleepy cattle rancher than a financial titan.

25
00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:01.040
But underneath that calm Texas drawl was his father's absolute obsession with hard assets.

26
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:06.340
Bunker inherited a personal fortune in oil fields, massive cattle ranches, and prize-winning racehorses.

27
00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:10.350
But in nineteen seventy-three, disaster struck.

28
00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:11.930
Bunker had invested heavily in a massive oil field in Libya.

29
00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:14.790
Out of nowhere, Colonel Muammar Gaddafi led a military coup,

30
00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:19.220
seized power, and nationalized Bunker's entire oil operation without paying him a single dime.

31
00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.520
The Libyan seizure shook Bunker Hunt to his core.

32
00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:27.290
He realized that a government could wipe out decades of work with the stroke of a pen.

33
00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:32.770
At the exact same time, back home in America, inflation was spiraling out of control.

34
00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:37.520
President Richard Nixon had just severed the final link between the dollar and gold.

35
00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:44.340
Prices for bread, milk, and gasoline were soaring, and paper money seemed to be melting away like ice under the Texas sun.

36
00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:50.930
Bunker turned to his brother Herbert and told him that if they kept their wealth in American dollars,

37
00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:53.040
they were going to end up broke.

38
00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.180
They needed something real.

39
00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:57.270
Naturally, the first thing the Hunt brothers wanted to buy was gold.

40
00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:01.270
Gold had been the ultimate store of value for thousands of years.

41
00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:04.300
But there was a gigantic legal roadblock.

42
00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:09.070
Ever since President Franklin Roosevelt signed an executive order during the Great Depression,

43
00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:13.360
it was strictly illegal for private American citizens to own gold bullion.

44
00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:18.150
The government would throw you in federal prison if you hoarded gold bars in your basement.

45
00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:21.720
So Bunker and Herbert began looking for an alternative.

46
00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:26.080
And sitting right in plain sight was gold's forgotten little brother: silver.

47
00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.680
Silver was completely legal for any American citizen to buy and hold.

48
00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:31.860
Even better, it was shockingly cheap.

49
00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:39.210
Throughout the early nineteen seventies, silver traded for around one dollar and fifty cents an ounce.

50
00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:42.660
But Bunker Hunt was not just looking at the price tag.

51
00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.340
He started studying the global supply chain, and what he discovered blew his mind.

52
00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:52.440
Back then, the world was consuming far more silver every single year than all the silver mines on Earth were digging up.

53
00:03:52.440 --> 00:04:01.790
The photographic giant Eastman Kodak was using millions of ounces of silver every year to manufacture camera film.

54
00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:02.960
Electronics manufacturers needed silver for electrical switches.

55
00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:06.160
Medical companies needed silver for X-ray sheets.

56
00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:13.050
The gap between supply and demand was being filled by governments dumping their old silver coins and stockpiles into the market.

57
00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:16.360
But those government reserves were running dry.

58
00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:19.460
Bunker Hunt ran the numbers and came to an astonishing conclusion.

59
00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:23.470
If someone with enough cash stepped into the market and bought up the remaining supply,

60
00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:27.610
the entire global industrial machine would have to come begging to them for metal.

61
00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:33.520
In nineteen seventy-three, the Hunt brothers made their first move.

62
00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:38.540
They quietly purchased twenty million ounces of physical silver at roughly three dollars an ounce.

63
00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:41.180
But the brothers knew their own fortune, as vast as it was,

64
00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:44.520
could only push the market so far.

65
00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.750
They needed allies with deep pockets and the exact same hatred for paper currency.

66
00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:51.490
So Bunker Hunt boarded an airplane and flew straight to the Middle East.

67
00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:58.340
He met with members of the Saudi royal family, including Prince Mohammed bin Fahd.

68
00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:00.580
Bunker laid out his theory.

69
00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:02.930
He told the Saudis that Western paper currencies were doomed to collapse from runaway inflation,

70
00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:08.620
and that together, they could pool their petrodollars and build an unbeatable position in real silver.

71
00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:12.830
The Saudis loved the idea.

72
00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:15.220
Together, they formed a secret offshore partnership called the International Metals Investment Company.

73
00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:20.840
The trap was set, and the squeeze was about to begin.

74
00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:24.380
To understand how the Hunt brothers pulled off their scheme,

75
00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:27.440
you have to understand how commodity futures exchanges actually work.

76
00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:35.850
Normally, when big hedge funds or corporations trade silver on exchanges like the Chicago Board of Trade or the New York Commodity Exchange,

77
00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:38.360
nobody actually expects to see a single ounce of real metal.

78
00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:41.710
Instead, traders buy futures contracts.

79
00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:48.230
A futures contract is simply a legal agreement to buy a certain amount of silver at a specific price on a future date.

80
00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:53.600
But when that delivery date arrives, ninety-nine percent of traders do not want heavy silver bars dumped on their front lawn.

81
00:05:53.600 --> 00:06:00.530
They simply settle the contract in cash, take their paper profit or loss,

82
00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:02.190
and roll over into the next month.

83
00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:04.140
The Hunt brothers, however, had zero interest in paper profits.

84
00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:08.460
They did not want paper cash.

85
00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:08.660
They wanted the metal.

86
00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:12.910
When their contracts expired, the Hunts did something that sent shockwaves through the trading pits.

87
00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:14.840
They demanded physical delivery.

88
00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:18.250
They told the exchange: do not wire us dollars, bring us our silver.

89
00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:25.420
Suddenly, armored trucks began backing up to exchange warehouses across New York and Chicago,

90
00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:30.550
loading up thousands of heavy thirty-two-pound bars of solid silver.

91
00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:34.200
But Bunker Hunt was terrified the United States government might try to seize his metal,

92
00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:38.710
just like Roosevelt had seized gold forty years earlier.

93
00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:41.880
So he decided to take the silver completely out of the country.

94
00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:43.260
In the dead of winter, under the cover of darkness,

95
00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:45.760
Bunker Hunt chartered three Boeing seven hundred and seven cargo jets.

96
00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:52.450
He hired a squad of armed Texas ranch hands, dressed in cowboy boots and heavy jackets,

97
00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:55.130
to guard the loading docks.

98
00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:57.200
Pallet after pallet of solid silver bullion was loaded onto the planes.

99
00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:03.230
The jets took off into the night and flew straight across the Atlantic Ocean,

100
00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:04.190
touching down in Zurich and London.

101
00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:09.080
The silver was quietly locked away inside deep Swiss bank vaults.

102
00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:13.150
By nineteen seventy-nine, the Hunts and their Saudi partners were not just buying silver with cash.

103
00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:14.900
They were using leverage.

104
00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:20.920
They would take their physical silver bars, pledge them as collateral to borrow millions of dollars from Wall Street banks,

105
00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:26.750
and then use that borrowed cash to buy even more futures contracts.

106
00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:30.380
It was an unstoppable compounding loop.

107
00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:33.370
By late nineteen seventy-nine, the Hunt brothers controlled an estimated two hundred million ounces of silver.

108
00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:39.470
That was roughly half of all the deliverable silver in the entire Western world.

109
00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:44.310
With hundreds of millions of ounces locked away in private vaults,

110
00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:47.120
the open market suddenly experienced an extreme shortage.

111
00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:52.610
Industrial companies that desperately needed silver for film, electronics, and medicine could not find enough bars to buy.

112
00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:56.840
The price of silver went completely berserk.

113
00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:59.610
In January of nineteen seventy-nine, silver was trading around six dollars an ounce.

114
00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:02.730
By September, it hit eleven dollars.

115
00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:04.800
By December, it blew past twenty-five dollars.

116
00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:10.900
And then, in the first two weeks of January nineteen eighty, the market entered full-blown speculative hysteria.

117
00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:20.640
On January eighteenth, nineteen eighty, the price of silver touched an all-time record high of fifty dollars and thirty-five cents an ounce.

118
00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:23.140
In just one year, the price had skyrocketed by more than seven hundred percent.

119
00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:29.360
On paper, Bunker and Herbert Hunt were now sitting on an unrealized profit of several billion dollars.

120
00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:32.800
They were, for a few brief weeks, arguably the wealthiest private individuals on Earth.

121
00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:38.680
Across the United States, regular citizens went into a state of total mania.

122
00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:48.830
People suddenly realized that the dusty antique tea sets sitting in their dining rooms and the silver spoons inherited from their grandparents were worth thousands of dollars.

123
00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:57.080
Lines stretched around the block outside jewelry shops, pawn shops, and coin dealers in every major American city.

124
00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:00.680
People stood in the freezing winter cold for hours, carrying pillowcases stuffed with silverware,

125
00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:06.200
communion plates, and even silver dental fillings, waiting to have them weighed and paid out in cash.

126
00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:13.490
Refineries worked around the clock, melting down irreplaceable nineteenth-century heirlooms into crude silver bricks.

127
00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:20.110
Burglars began breaking into suburban homes, ignoring televisions and stereo equipment,

128
00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:22.470
and heading straight for the dining room sideboard to steal the silverware.

129
00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:31.880
The iconic luxury jeweler Tiffany and Company was so outraged by the skyrocketing price that their chairman took out a full-page advertisement in The New York Times.

130
00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:39.730
The ad publicly blasted the Hunt brothers, calling their actions unconscionable and accusing them of hoarding a vital public resource for private greed.

131
00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:45.010
But Bunker Hunt simply laughed off the criticism.

132
00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:48.390
When a reporter asked him how it felt to risk his fortune on silver,

133
00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:51.830
Bunker calmly replied that a billion dollars just was not what it used to be.

134
00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:58.980
While the Hunt brothers were celebrating their paper billions, panic was sweeping through the executive suites of Wall Street and the commodity exchanges.

135
00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:07.060
The leaders of the Chicago Board of Trade and the Commodity Exchange in New York were staring into a financial abyss.

136
00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:14.330
Many of the exchange board members and major trading firms were on the short side of the trade,

137
00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:17.430
meaning they had bet that the price of silver would go down.

138
00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:24.620
If the Hunts continued demanding physical delivery, there literally was not enough silver on planet Earth to satisfy the contracts.

139
00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:31.290
The short sellers would go bankrupt, clearinghouses would fail, and the entire commodities trading system could collapse.

140
00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:35.380
So the men in charge decided to do the only thing that could save them.

141
00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:38.860
They changed the rules of the game in the middle of the match.

142
00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:43.080
First, the exchanges dramatically increased the margin requirements.

143
00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:50.360
Previously, an investor only needed to put down a few thousand dollars in cash to control a hundred-thousand-dollar contract of silver.

144
00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:56.670
The exchanges suddenly demanded that traders put up tens of thousands of dollars in cash for every single contract.

145
00:10:56.670 --> 00:10:58.750
Then came the kill shot.

146
00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:03.780
The exchanges enacted a brand-new emergency regulation known as liquidation only.

147
00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:09.020
Under this new rule, nobody in the world was allowed to buy new silver futures contracts.

148
00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:13.080
You were only allowed to sell existing contracts to close them out.

149
00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:13.280
Think about that for a second.

150
00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:15.520
Imagine an auction house where buyers are banned from making any bids,

151
00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:19.110
and sellers are only allowed to sell.

152
00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:23.640
With buyers legally kicked out of the room, there was only one direction the price could possibly go.

153
00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:26.800
To make matters worse, the Federal Reserve stepped in.

154
00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:34.850
Chairman Paul Volcker, who was aggressively battling national inflation, issued a direct warning to commercial banks across the country.

155
00:11:34.850 --> 00:11:38.800
He told them to immediately stop extending credit and loans for speculative commodity hoarding.

156
00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:44.240
Overnight, the Hunt brothers' financial pipeline was completely shut off.

157
00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:48.690
They could no longer borrow fresh cash against their silver,

158
00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:51.910
and the market they thought they controlled was suddenly rigged against them.

159
00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:57.530
As soon as the new rules took effect, the price of silver began to slide backward.

160
00:11:57.530 --> 00:12:00.160
From fifty dollars, it dropped to forty. Then thirty. Then twenty-five.

161
00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:04.810
Because the Hunt brothers had built their empire on borrowed money,

162
00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:10.070
every dollar drop in the price of silver triggered an avalanche of margin calls.

163
00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:18.560
A margin call is when a broker calls you on the telephone and demands that you deposit millions of dollars in fresh cash immediately to cover your losses.

164
00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:24.130
If you cannot produce the cash, the broker has the legal right to sell your assets at any price they can get.

165
00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:28.180
For several weeks, the Hunts scrambled.

166
00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:31.110
They poured in millions of dollars of their own cash,

167
00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:33.810
sold off family assets, and tried to negotiate new loans.

168
00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:35.150
But the decline was relentless.

169
00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:39.770
Everything came to a catastrophic head on the morning of March twenty-seventh,

170
00:12:39.770 --> 00:12:44.450
nineteen eighty, a day that financial history will forever remember as Silver Thursday.

171
00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:57.040
That morning, the major brokerage firm Bache Halsey Stuart Shields picked up the telephone and issued a massive margin call to the Hunt brothers for one hundred million dollars.

172
00:12:57.040 --> 00:12:57.240
The Hunts simply did not have the cash.

173
00:12:57.240 --> 00:13:03.950
Bunker Hunt was overseas in Europe trying to arrange emergency financing, but the clock ran out.

174
00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:05.540
Bache immediately began liquidating the Hunts' silver positions on the open floor,

175
00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:09.970
dumping millions of ounces onto a market that had zero buyers.

176
00:13:09.970 --> 00:13:13.410
The trading pits erupted in absolute pandemonium.

177
00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:19.250
The price of silver went into freefall, plunging from twenty-one dollars down to ten dollars and eighty cents in a matter of hours.

178
00:13:19.250 --> 00:13:30.440
Rumors spread like wildfire across Wall Street that major brokerage firms were failing and that the Hunt brothers were insolvent.

179
00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:32.860
The Dow Jones Industrial Average plunged, and trading in several banking stocks was temporarily halted.

180
00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:44.580
Terrified of a systemic banking collapse, Federal Reserve officials and a syndicate of thirteen major commercial banks rushed into emergency meetings.

181
00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:53.830
Over the coming weeks, they put together a one-point-one-billion-dollar emergency credit package to bail out the brokerage firms and prevent a domino collapse of the financial system.

182
00:13:53.830 --> 00:13:55.230
But in exchange, the banks took total control of the Hunts.

183
00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:57.820
The brothers were forced to pledge their vast oil properties,

184
00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:02.860
real estate, ranches, racehorses, and family holdings as collateral.

185
00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:04.960
The silver empire had turned into a financial prison.

186
00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:08.550
The fallout from the silver squeeze dragged on for nearly a decade.

187
00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:20.330
In nineteen eighty-eight, a federal jury found Nelson Bunker Hunt and William Herbert Hunt guilty of civil conspiracy to corner the silver market.

188
00:14:20.330 --> 00:14:28.630
The jury ordered the brothers to pay over one hundred and thirty million dollars in damages to a Peruvian state-owned mineral company that had been caught in the crossfire.

189
00:14:28.630 --> 00:14:33.950
Faced with astronomical legal bills, bank debts, and penalties from the Internal Revenue Service,

190
00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:38.620
both Bunker and Herbert Hunt were forced to file for Chapter eleven personal bankruptcy.

191
00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:46.340
At their bankruptcy hearings, Bunker Hunt sat quietly in a crowded courtroom while auctioneers sold off his prize racehorses,

192
00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:49.860
his father's oil leases, and even his personal coin collections to satisfy creditors.

193
00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:56.130
His personal fortune, once estimated to be well over five billion dollars, had almost completely vanished.

194
00:14:56.130 --> 00:15:05.610
Decades later, the Hunt brothers' silver gamble stands as one of the most audacious attempts in history to corner a global commodity.

195
00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:06.620
They were right about inflation.

196
00:15:06.620 --> 00:15:09.810
They were right that industrial demand was outstripping mine supply.

197
00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:14.220
And they were right that paper currency loses purchasing power over time.

198
00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:20.420
But they forgot the most important rule of high-stakes finance: when the little guy breaks the rules,

199
00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:22.450
he goes to jail.

200
00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:25.610
But when the big guy breaks the house, the house simply rewrites the rules.

